The {{nihongo|'''Siege of Ichijōdani'''|一乗谷の役|Ichijōdani no Eki|extra=or, more commonly, 一乗谷の陣 ''Ichijōdani no Jin''}} was a series of battles undertaken by the [[Honda shogunate]] against the [[Ii clan]], and ending with the deaths of the leaders of both clans as well as the so-called "Great Reconciliation". The siege put an end to the last major armed opposition to the shogunate's establishment and is widely regarded as the emotional and psychological end of the [[Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Sengoku period]] in [[Okaiken]].

==Background==

===The Dual Sovereignty and the Kinzō Reforms===
{{Main|Dual Sovereignty Period|Kinzō reforms}}

The political system that produced the siege was the {{nihongo|Dual Sovereignty|両界の令|Ryōkai-no-Rei}}, established by [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]] (Honda Shigetada) in 1611. Under this arrangement, the Emperor retained sovereign authority over diplomacy, religious rites, and high appointments, while the title of Shogun was delegated to his second son, [[Honda Shigetomo]], who administered the ''bakufu'' (military government) from [[Okami]]. This division of power had brought stability to the war-torn nation, enabling the pacification of the remaining independent lords: the [[Chōsokabe clan]] surrendered in July 1610, the [[Shimazu clan]] in 1612, and the recalcitrant [[Hōsō clan]] in 1618, completing military reunification.

However, the Dual Sovereignty also created a structural vulnerability: the bureaucratic distance between the Emperor's strategic vision and the Shogun's operational enforcement. This gap widened dramatically after 1619, when Shirakawa formally retired to [[Toyozawa Castle]] as a Cloistered Emperor (''Insei''). From this stronghold, he and Shigetomo drafted the controversial [[Kinzō reforms]], which nationalized land surveying and allowed the shogunate to forcefully relocate daimyōs to dilute their regional power. While the reforms were designed to prevent another civil war like the [[Battle of Tone]], they were interpreted by the ''fudai'' daimyōs as an existential threat to their ancestral territories.

===Ii Naomasa and the broken bridge===
{{Main|Ii Naomasa}}
[[File:Ii Naomasa Red Devil Sashimono.png|thumb|left|200px|The preserved crimson ''sashimono'' battle standard of the Ii clan's Red Devil cavalry, displayed at the Honda Shrine in Okami Castle. The standard was retired from service after Naomasa's death in 1604.]]
The relationship between the Honda and the Ii clans had been the military backbone of the early Shogunate. [[Ii Naomasa]], known as {{nihongo|"The Red Devil"|赤鬼|Aka-oni}} for the crimson-lacquered armor worn by his elite cavalry, was one of the [[Honda Shitennō|Four Honda Heavenly Pillars]] and the most feared battlefield commander of his generation. Discovered by Honda Shigetada in a Nagashino weapons shop in 1589, Naomasa had pledged his family to the Honda cause, sending his young children—including the future rebel [[Ii Naotaka]]—as hostages to [[Otomo Castle]] to prove his sincerity.

Naomasa died on May 21, 1604, from chronic infection of a gunshot wound sustained during the Rengoshu Pacification campaigns. His death removed the primary bridge of trust between the Honda and the Ii clans. Naomasa was the only man who possessed both the unshakable loyalty of Shigetada and the fierce love of his son Naotaka. He understood Shigetada's strategic patience from the inside—and he understood, from his years as a rōnin, the terror of a clan that fears it is about to be erased. Without Naomasa to translate between these two worlds, the relationship between the shogunate and the Ii clan was conducted entirely through bureaucratic channels—edicts, land surveys, and relocation orders—each one interpreted by the Ii as a potential death sentence.

Naomasa's final words to Naotaka were a warning: "Your lord is a man of great heart. But the world is not made of hearts. It is made of swords and rice. Remember this when the time comes." This warning—combined with the deep ideological gulf between Naomasa's philosophy of decisive action and Shigetada's philosophy of strategic leniency—would haunt the Ii clan for the next two decades, culminating in the events at Ichijōdani.

===Ii Naotaka and the road to rebellion===
{{Main|Ii Naotaka}}

Ii Naotaka, childhood name {{nihongo|Manchiyo|万千代}}, had spent his formative years alongside Shigetomo as a political hostage at Otomo Castle. The two boys formed a deep, brotherly bond during their shared captivity, relying on one another to survive the psychological strain of being leverage for their respective clans. Their imprisonment lasted until March 1592, when a betrayal by [[Takigawa Kazumasu]] allowed Honda forces to assault Otomo Castle and liberate the hostages.

For over two decades, Naotaka served as a vital pillar of the Honda Shogunate. He was elevated to the status of a premier ''fudai'' daimyō, acting as a trusted confidant and military enforcer for Shigetomo after the latter was invested as the second Shogun in 1610. However, the unbreakable bond between the two men began to fracture in the early 1620s. The Kinzō reforms proposed transferring the Ii from their ancestral home in Ichijoudani to a new, highly lucrative one-million koku territory in Bungo (modern-day [[Ofunato prefecture]]). Naotaka, influenced by conservative elements within his clan and terrified that the transfer was actually a trap designed to strip the Ii of their defensible lands before executing them, began to quietly resist the edicts.

The bureaucratic frenzy and Shigetomo's increasing paranoia led to a severe breakdown in communication. On 28 May 1625, Shigetomo sent Naotaka a formal inquiry asking if everything was in order regarding the clan's impending transfer. Naotaka responded respectfully on 30 May, assuring the Shogun that the Ii clan was preparing for their relocation. However, other letters from this period show Shigetomo writing to his brother, [[Emperor Daigo of Mitoyagi|Emperor Daigo]], expressing deep paranoia that the outer clans were secretly amassing weapons to resist the edicts. The two men—once inseparable—were now trapped in a spiral of mutual suspicion.

==Prelude: The Kinzō Incident==
{{Main|Kinzō Incident}}

The situation reached a boiling point on the night of 6 June 1625. Convinced that the Honda Shogunate intended to destroy them, a coalition of disgruntled vassals rallied behind Naotaka. The Ii forces, aided by internal traitors from the [[Ogasawara clan]], breached the outer walls of [[Toyozawa Castle]] under the cover of a thunderstorm.

===Assassination of Emperor Shirakawa===
[[File:Burning of Toyozawa.png|thumb|left|280px|''The Night of Blue Fire'', a 17th-century woodblock print depicting the burning of Toyozawa Castle during the Kinzō Incident.]]
Contemporary accounts describe the death of the Retired Emperor as a "battle, not an assassination." Despite being 69 years old, Shirakawa donned his ceremonial armor and wielded his legendary spear, ''[[Tonbogiri]]''. Reports state that he held the bridge to the Inner Sanctum alone for nearly an hour, allowing his female attendants and the young Prince Honda Shigezane to escape. Shirakawa reportedly slew over thirty attackers before his spear shaft was shattered by musket fire.

Confronted by Naotaka in the burning courtyard, Shirakawa refused to commit ''seppuku''. He declared that he was the Emperor, and only Heaven could command his death. Unwilling to engage his former lord in single combat, Naotaka turned his back, allowing a squad of ''ashigaru'' arquebusiers to open fire. Shirakawa was struck by seven bullets and died standing, propped up by the remains of his spear. The head was not taken—an act of strange mercy by Naotaka—but the burning of Toyozawa Castle sent shockwaves across the nation.

[[File:Tonbogiri Spear Remains.png|thumb|right|250px|The shattered remains of the ''Tonbogiri'' spear, recovered from the ruins of Toyozawa Castle. The shaft was broken by musket fire during Shirakawa's last stand. Now displayed at the Imperial Museum in Mito.]]

===Shigetomo's breakdown===
[[File:Shigetomo at Toyozawa ruins.png|thumb|right|250px|A 17th-century handscroll depiction of Shigetomo discovering the body of Emperor Shirakawa in the ruins of Toyozawa Castle.]]
Shigetomo's vanguard reached the smoking ruins of Toyozawa on the morning of 8 June. Upon entering the charred remains of the Inner Sanctum, he discovered the body of his father. Witnesses claim that Shigetomo did not weep. Instead, he fell into a catatonic state for several hours, sitting silently beside his father's corpse amidst the ash. When he finally stood, his demeanor had shifted from the rational, reformist bureaucrat to the erratic, vengeance-fueled "Punisher" of his youth.

He ordered the summary execution of the castle guards who had survived, blaming them for his father's death. He then refused to return to Okami to coordinate a proper response. Instead, he declared that he would "hunt the Red Devils to the ends of the earth," mobilizing the [[Hotta]] and [[Okudaira]] clans for an immediate, ill-advised pursuit. According to the ''[[Mitsukoji]]'', by the night of 9 June, Shigetomo was consuming alcohol in heavy quantities, his calligraphy becoming noticeably rushed with frequent ink splatters and crossed-out sentences. He engaged in a frantic, contradictory exchange of orders with his loyalist commanders—one moment ordering the razing of any village harboring Ii sympathizers, the next countermanding the order hours later.

==Outset==
Following the assassination of [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]] (Honda Shigetada) during the [[Kinzō Incident]] on June 6, 1625, the grieving Shogun [[Honda Shigetomo]] mobilized a massive punitive force. Despite the advice of his generals to wait for heavy artillery, Shigetomo ordered an immediate march on the Ii stronghold. The decision was characteristic of the man who had earned the moniker {{nihongo|"The Punisher"|お仕置き|Oshioki}} in his youth—a leader who believed in overwhelming preemptive force. But it was also the decision of a man in the grip of profound psychological collapse: a son who had just learned that his father had been killed by his childhood friend.

The Shogunate forces, numbering over 70,000, converged on the narrow valley of Ichijōdani in [[Ise Prefecture]], where [[Ii Naotaka]] had retreated with fewer than 7,000 men. The ten-to-one numerical advantage was, on paper, overwhelming. However, the forces that assembled were not the disciplined professional army of the early Shogunate. Shigetomo's erratic behavior—heavy drinking, contradictory orders, a physical altercation with his second-in-command [[Hotta Muneshige]] on 16 June—severely compromised the command structure. Second-in-command Hotta Muneshige took operational control of the Hotta and Okudaira contingents, deploying them at Minatokoriyama approximately 8 kilometres northeast of Ichijōdani Castle, while Shigetomo remained at the main base at the foot of Mount Hei.

The Ii clan, aware of their numerical disadvantage, destroyed the bridges across the Ichijōdani River and fortified the western banks, hoping to use the torrential monsoon rains to stall the Honda advance. Naotaka's defense strategy reflected the martial traditions of his father, Ii Naomasa, whose "Red Devil" cavalry had been built on the principle of disciplined ferocity against superior numbers. But Naotaka was not his father. Where Naomasa would have charged the gates, Naotaka chose to dig in—a decision born of desperation and the knowledge that the Ii could not survive a war of attrition.

==Battle of the Ichijōdani Rapids==
[[File:Battle of Ichijoudani Rapids.png|thumb|right|300px|A 17th-century woodblock print depicting the Battle of the Ichijōdani Rapids, with Honda vanguard troops exposed to Ii musket fire as the fog lifts over the river.]]
Hostilities commenced on the morning of June 12. Honda vanguard commander Manabe Hachisuke attempted to ford the swollen river under cover of fog. However, the Ii musketeers, commanded by Kimura Shigetora, had pre-sighted the crossing points. When the fog lifted, the Honda vanguard was exposed to withering enfilade fire—a devastating demonstration of the massed arquebus tactics that had transformed Sanese warfare since the [[Battle of Tone]].

Manabe Hachisuke was killed in the first volley, and the Honda advance collapsed in confusion. Shigetomo, observing from Mount Hei, reportedly executed two of his own captains for cowardice, further damaging the morale of the Shogunate army. The executions were a grim echo of the "Punisher" persona that Shigetomo had spent a decade trying to outgrow—the man who had once been unable to sheathe his sword due to the blood caked on the blade. This initial engagement proved that despite the numbers, the Ii defenses were formidable, and that the Shogun's erratic leadership was a liability.

==Battle of Nishi-ku==
[[File:Battle of Nishiku Ichijoudani.png|thumb|left|280px|A 17th-century handscroll depicting the urban combat in the Nishi-ku merchant quarter, with Ii troops evacuating civilians through burning streets.]]
By June 14, the rains had subsided, allowing the main Honda force under [[Hotta Muneshige]] to construct pontoon bridges. The Shogunate army crossed the river in force, overwhelming the Ii outer defenses through sheer weight of numbers. The fighting moved into Nishi-ku (West District), the merchant quarter outside the main castle walls.

The battle descended into brutal urban combat—the kind of house-to-house fighting that had characterized the worst engagements of the Sengoku period. Ii Naotaka ordered his troops to fight for every house to buy time for civilians to evacuate into the castle keep. The fighting lasted for two days. On June 16, the Ii general Kimura Shigetora launched a suicide charge to halt the advance of the Okudaira clan, allowing the remaining Ii forces to retreat behind the castle walls. Shigetora was killed, but his sacrifice stabilized the Ii defensive line and allowed the civilian population of Ichijōdani to reach the safety of the keep—a decision that reflected Naotaka's desperate attempt to preserve something of the honor that had driven his father's generation.

==Castle Siege==
[[File:Ichijoudani Castle Siege Night.png|thumb|right|280px|A 17th-century handscroll depicting the night stalemate before the Great Reconciliation, with Shigetomo alone in his tent as Ichijōdani Castle looms in the rain.]]
By June 17, [[Ichijōdani Castle]] was completely surrounded. Shigetomo established his command post just outside the main gate, well within the range of Ii cannons, displaying a reckless disregard for his own safety—a behavior that his retainers recognized as the same death-seeking impulse that had defined the "Punisher" of his youth. He ordered a general assault at noon, but his second-in-command, Hotta Muneshige, fearing a massacre, held back the reserves. The assault failed, leaving thousands of Honda dead in the mud.

That night, a tense stalemate settled over the valley. Inside the castle, Ii Naotaka contemplated suicide to spare his men from a massacre—the same impulse that had driven his father's generation to sacrifice everything for their lord. Outside, Shigetomo descended into an alcohol-fueled psychosis, hallucinating the ghost of his father. According to the ''[[Mitsukoji]]'', Shigetomo sat alone in his tent, unable to sleep, muttering that "there is no Shogun here—only a son who let his father burn." It was during this night that Shigetomo penned the final, bloodstained letter to Naotaka—a letter that would transform the meaning of the battle and the future of the nation.

==\"Great Reconciliation\"==
[[File:Great Reconciliation Ichijoudani.png|thumb|left|300px|''The Great Reconciliation'', a 17th-century woodblock print depicting Naotaka cradling the dying Shigetomo in the mud outside Ichijōdani Castle. From the Heianji Temple collection.]]
The "Great Reconciliation" ({{nihongo|Dai-Wakai|大和解}}) refers to the events of the morning of June 18, 1625. It is considered one of the most pivotal and tragic moments in the history of Okaiken, marking the emotional end of the Sengoku mindset. The events of that morning have been the subject of intense historiographical debate for centuries, with competing narratives reflecting the political needs of successive eras.

===Accepted historical position===
For centuries, official Shogunate records stated that Honda Shigetomo approached the castle to negotiate a surrender. While attempting to exchange terms, a "stray shot" from an undisciplined rebel soldier struck the Shogun. Ii Naotaka, witnessing the dishonorable act, rushed out to apologize. He then committed ''seppuku'' to atone for his subordinate's crime, and Shigetomo succumbed to his wounds shortly after. This version paints both men as victims of circumstance.

===Events from the ''Mitsukoji''===
The [[Mitsukoji]] chronicle, fully declassified in 2016, reveals a more personal tragedy. It states that Shigetomo rode out alone, wearing full armor, screaming for his childhood friend "Manchiyo" (Naotaka) to face him. He intended to provoke a duel but secretly carried a letter of forgiveness.

When Naotaka refused to fight, Shigetomo reached into his armor for the letter. An Ii sniper, mistaking the movement for a weapon, fired. Shigetomo was mortally wounded. Naotaka rushed to his side, and upon reading the bloodstained letter offering him the Bungo domain, realized the war was a misunderstanding. In a pact of brotherhood, Naotaka committed ''seppuku'' beside the dying Shogun so they could "sleep in the same earth."

===Forensic reconstruction===
[[File:Shigetomo armor Ichijoudani.png|thumb|right|250px|Honda Shigetomo's damaged ''o-yoroi'' armor and bloodstained ''shitagi'' (undershirt), displayed at the National Museum of History in Okami. The smaller entry hole from the 15mm matchlock ball is visible on the lower left plate.]]
Modern forensic analysis of Shigetomo's preserved armor and the battlefield artifacts has overturned much of the accepted historical narrative. On the early morning of 18 June, Shigetomo dismounted his horse approximately twenty meters from the main gate of Ichijōdani Castle before any shots were fired. As he stood alone in the mud, a 15-millimeter lead ball fired from an Ii matchlock struck him. Contrary to the traditional belief of an upper chest wound, forensic analysis indicates an oblique entry into the lower left torso, penetrating the external oblique muscle and critically lacerating the liver and branches of the portal vein.

Within twenty seconds of the impact, Shigetomo collapsed. His knees buckled, and he pitched forward into the mud. Recognizing the catastrophic error, Naotaka sprinted from the gate to the fallen Shogun, rotating his body from a prone to a supine position and cradling his childhood friend's head and shoulders in his lap. Shortly thereafter, Hotta Muneshige arrived from the Honda encampment and knelt beside them, securing Shigetomo's left hand across his chest plate. It was at this moment that Naotaka reached inside Shigetomo's armor and retrieved two folded, bloodstained letters, reading them aloud. Shigetomo, experiencing rapid hypovolemic shock, was largely unresponsive, managing only faint, wet rasps. Approximately one minute and forty seconds after the shot, Shigetomo entered the agonal phase. His breathing ceased entirely at the two-minute mark, and clinical death followed moments later.

===The Honda letters===
{{Main|Honda letters}}
[[File:Honda Letters Museum.png|thumb|left|280px|The two Honda letters addressed to Ii Naotaka, on display at the National Museum of History in Okami. The bloodstained second letter (right) suffered severe elemental damage and required two years of restoration.]]
On April 8, 2016, the Heianji temple, in association with the University of Okami and the Toho Shuheisha University, released the so-called "Honda letters"—a collection of correspondence that Honda Shigetomo wrote from 18 May to 17 June 1625. The centerpiece of the collection features the two final documents both addressed to Ii Naotaka. The first letter, found crumpled and unsent, is wildly aggressive, demanding that Naotaka surrender his head immediately or face dire consequences. The second version, however, is a complete reversal in tone. This was the letter Shigetomo carried onto the battlefield on the day of his death. In it, Shigetomo addressed Naotaka with profound vulnerability, stating that he had forgiven his childhood friend's actions and was prepared to do everything in his power to win back their friendship. It also contained a formal reassurance that the Ii domain in Higo, valued at one million koku, would be honored. This final, bloodstained letter suffered severe elemental damage and took the university research team almost two years to fully decipher, standing today as the ultimate testament to the tragic Great Reconciliation.

===Honda clan witnesses===
[[Hotta Muneshige]] and the monk [[Sessai Taigen]] were the first to reach the bodies. Muneshige, who had served as Shigetomo's second-in-command throughout the siege—and who had been physically assaulted by the Shogun during a drunken altercation on 16 June—recorded that Shigetomo's final words were the famous directive: {{nihongo|"The true enemy was my very own existence. Let everyone come to my grave and rethink of their existence and seal it."|戒むべきは実在なり、我が墓に詣でる者をもう必ず意味実在を封じて考えろっじゃ|extra=excerpt from the ''Mitsukoji'', page 774}} Before this, Shigetomo had reportedly gasped: "Manchiyo, Yachiman... I am sorry. It was all my fault." Muneshige acted as the second for Naotaka's suicide, honoring the Shogun's wish that the Ii lord die as a samurai, not a rebel.

===Ii clan witnesses===
[[Ii Naoyoshi]], Naotaka's son, watched from the ramparts. He later testified that his father threw away his sword before running to Shigetomo. Naoyoshi preserved the bloodstained letter, which became the Ii clan's most sacred treasure, proving that the Honda Shogun had forgiven them before his death. Naoyoshi's acceptance of the terms of surrender and his subsequent stewardship of the Ii clan ensured that they became one of the most staunchly loyal political forces during the remainder of the Honda Period, eventually culminating in the establishment of the modern [[Ikyo]] technology conglomerate.

==Aftermath==
The deaths of both leaders shocked the armies into an immediate ceasefire. Hotta Muneshige and Honda Shigetoki negotiated with Ii Naoyoshi in the hours following the double death, producing the terms that would define the next two and a half centuries of Sanese governance.

* '''Amnesty''': The Ii clan was spared annihilation. They surrendered the northern strategic half of Ichijoudani but retained the southern half. The Ii were stripped of more than 70,000 ''koku'' in war reparations, but the survival of the clan was itself a revolutionary act in an era where defeated enemies were typically erased.
* '''Succession''': [[Honda Shigetoki]], Shigetomo's scholarly half-brother, became the 3rd Shogun. Shigetoki's temperament—measured, diplomatic, and conciliatory—was precisely the opposite of his brother's volatile intensity, and his succession represented the permanent transition from the Sengoku warrior ethos to the administrative state.
* '''Political Shift''': The tragedy effectively ended the resistance of the ''tozama'' daimyos, who were moved by the story of the "Double Suicide." It solidified the legitimacy of the Honda Shogunate not through force, but through shared grief. Shigetoki honored his brother's dying wish by issuing the Edict of Clemency, and the Kinzō reforms were continued slowly and with greater sensitivity.
* '''Strategic Necessity''': The decision to spare the Ii was not merely an act of mercy. To the south, the nation of [[Nakamizu]] had recently been reunified by the [[Kawaei clan]] under [[Kawaei Yoshiyuki]], ending their own century of civil war. With Kawaei forces aggressively expanding—deploying over 80,000 soldiers in April 1620 and another 25,000 in March 1621—the Honda Shogunate could not afford to execute its most capable generals. The Ii were pardoned but eventually demoted to the smaller domain of Kozuka, where they served as a vital buffer against potential southern aggression for the next 250 years.

===End of the Sengoku Mindset===
The siege of Ichijoudani is widely regarded by historians as the emotional and psychological terminus of the [[Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Sengoku period]]. While the [[Battle of Tone]] in 1600 had ended the military contest for supremacy, it was the Great Reconciliation that ended the Sengoku ''mindset''—the belief that political problems could and should be resolved through violence. When Shigetomo, the greatest warrior of his generation, died reaching for a letter of forgiveness rather than a weapon, and when Naotaka, the son of the Red Devil, chose to die beside his enemy rather than fight to the bitter end, the Sanese people recognized that the old way was dead. The collective trauma of the siege—the fear that chaos leads to extinction—remains a cornerstone of the national psyche to this day.

==Legacy==

===Ichijōdani today===
{{main|Ichijoudani}}
[[File:Ichijoudani Heianji Graves.png|thumb|right|280px|The opposing graves of Honda Shigetomo (left) and Ii Naotaka (right) at Heianji temple, Ichijoudani, connected by the annual red hemp rope during the Kanto festival.]]
Ichijoudani was rebuilt after the battle, but most of the services were shifted away from the battle site. What was once the town's central area was replaced by a temple, Shingo-ji, and commerce was moved further north to honour those who were killed. Shigetomo and Naotaka are buried on opposite sides of the Heianji temple, just across from the Shingo-ji temple, with one grave looking after another. On each anniversary of their deaths, now coinciding with the town's ''Kanto'' festival, both graves are connected by a hemp rope that symbolizes their friendship. A person wearing black clothing representing the "demon" then cuts both ropes, which leads to the opening of a gate to the spot in the temple courtyard where Shigetomo and Naotaka died.

Shigetomo is worshipped as the {{nihongo|"God of Seals"|封印の神|Fūin no Kami}}; thousands of messages praying for an end of their struggles are written on a small paddle, sealed with a red ink stamp, and placed as an offering to the temple, which is then burned to ashes at the annual festival. Naotaka is worshipped as the {{nihongo|"God of Vindication"|—}}; thousands of messages praying for hope of the future are written on a similar paddle but sealed with a blank stamp. These are thrown into the Ichijoudani River after seven days, where they flow to the Sea of Mitoyagi.

[[File:Ichijoudani Shamoji Paddles.png|thumb|left|250px|''Shamoji'' prayer paddles from the Heianji temple. The left paddle, sealed with a red stamp, is offered to Shigetomo (God of Seals); the right paddle, sealed with a blank stamp, is cast into the river for Naotaka (God of Vindication).]]

===Historiographical debate===
Honda Shigetomo's actions during the final hours of the siege remain one of the most hotly debated subjects in Sanese historiography. The central academic dispute revolves around whether Shigetomo's death was a calculated martyrdom or the chaotic result of an alcohol-fueled breakdown.

The '''Martyrdom Thesis''', championed by traditionalist historians, argues that Shigetomo, overwhelmed by the guilt of his father's death and recognizing that his own tyrannical Kinzō reforms had pushed the nation to the brink, deliberately sought a peaceful resolution. Proponents of this view point to the second Ii Letter, carried on his person, as definitive proof that he approached the gates fully intending to forgive Naotaka and offer his own life as a political pressure release valve to save the shogunate.

Conversely, the '''Provocation Thesis''', largely driven by revisionist scholars analyzing the erratic first drafts of his letters, suggests a much darker reality. These historians argue that Shigetomo marched to the gates in a genuine, homicidal rage, intending to force Naotaka into a duel or execute him on the spot. Under this interpretation, the forgiving letter was merely a discarded alternative drafted during a fleeting moment of sobriety, and Shigetomo's death was essentially a tragedy brought on by severe psychological collapse and the fatal misjudgment of an anxious foot soldier.

Regardless of his true internal state on that morning, historians unanimously agree that the consequences of his death were foundational to the modern Sanese state. By dying alongside Ii Naotaka, Shigetomo inadvertently created the political capital necessary for his brother, Honda Shigetoki, to enact the Great Reconciliation, successfully preserving the unified nation they had fought so violently to build.

===The Ii clan's continuation===
After the death of ''Tairō'' [[Ii Naoyoshi]] in 1864, the Ii family vacated their territory in Ichijoudani and were demoted to the territory of Kozuka, a 10,000 ''koku'' territory but nonetheless important in Sanese trade during the late Honda period. When the shogunate was dissolved and the ''han'' system abolished, the Ii clan became influential in establishing Ii Holdings Company in 1955. Ii Holdings later merged with Onkyo to form [[Ikyo]], one of the world's leading technology companies. The Honda clan, meanwhile, remained influential in 20th and 21st century politics, with [[Honda Tadashige]], [[Honda Shigehara]], and [[Honda Shigetsuma]] elected as [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Prime Ministers]] in 1961, 1991, and 2020, respectively.

==In popular culture==
The siege is the climax of the 1983 [[Taisen drama]] ''[[Honda Shigetomo (Taisen drama)|Honda Shigetomo]]''. The finale, depicting the "Great Reconciliation," achieved a 90.4% rating—the highest in Sanese television history. The event is also commemorated annually in the Ichijōdani {{nihongo|"Red String Festival"|赤糸祭|Akaito Matsuri}}. The siege also appears in the 2014 Taisen drama ''Honda Tenno'' and the 1965 Taisen drama ''[[Ii Naomasa]]'', which portrayed the Red Devil's early struggles and pivotal role in the Rengoshu Pacification, with the Ichijoudani tragedy as the haunting conclusion of the Ii family's arc.

